            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="proof">
    <p class="eyebrow">Get evidence</p>
    <h2>What proof of work looks like in e-commerce operations</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need a job title to start building proof that you understand this field. You need one concrete artefact tied to the function you actually want.
    </p>

    <div class="skills-grid">
      {proofCards.map((card) => (
        <article class="skill-card">
          <p class="mini-label">{card.label}</p>
          <h3>{card.title}</h3>
          <p>{card.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
      <p>
        A simple rule helps here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pick one function, build one small, real artefact inside it, and be ready to walk an interviewer through exactly how you built it and what you would change.
      </p>
      <p>
        That single piece of proof, explained clearly, beats a resume that just says "interested in e-commerce operations."
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="next-step">
    <p class="eyebrow">Action plan</p>
    <h2>What to do next if you are serious about e-commerce operations</h2>
    <p>
      First, pick one function from the six above — inventory/warehouse, last-mile logistics, marketplace/seller ops, catalog management, D2C fulfilment, or category/vendor management — instead of applying broadly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Second, pick your employer type deliberately. Marketplaces offer the most structured ladder, quick-commerce and D2C offer faster ownership and a steeper pace, and logistics-tech companies offer a direct route into the infrastructure side of the sector.
    </p>
    <p>
      Third, get hands-on with at least one relevant tool — a WMS or ERP trial, a walkthrough of a marketplace seller dashboard, or a small forecasting spreadsheet — before your first interview.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fourth, once you are in, ask for ownership of one real metric as early as you reasonably can, and document the before-and-after story once you move it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the decision pressure around this choice is real, explore the wider decision tools on the <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/career-skills-compass" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Career &amp; Skills Compass</a> or talk through your specific situation with <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
